\title{How fit() compares the simulation with the observations: the loss}
\description{
\code{\link[=fit]{fit()}} estimates the parameters by making the simulation match the
observations. "Match" is measured by the loss: for each of the six
responses -- \code{dbh}, \code{ba}, \code{trees}, \code{growth}, \code{mortality}, \code{regeneration} --
the simulated value of every site, census year and species is compared
with the observed one through a likelihood, the six comparisons are added
up, and \code{fit()} lowers that sum. This page explains the arguments of
\code{\link[=fit]{fit()}} that set the comparison: \code{loss_family}, \code{loss_aggregation},
\code{loss_resolution}, \code{loss_weights} and \code{weight_scale}, \code{loss_recruit}, and
\code{tree_data}. Their defaults are robust in most cases: a first fit needs
none of them.
}
\section{The six responses and their likelihoods (\code{loss_family})}{

Each response has its own likelihood, chosen for the kind of number it is.
The defaults, following Pichler & Käber (2026):\tabular{llll}{
   response \tab what is compared \tab default \tab alternatives \cr
   \code{dbh} \tab mean diameter, cm \tab \code{"mse"} (squared error) \tab \code{"gaussian"}, \code{"none"} \cr
   \code{ba} \tab basal area per patch \tab \code{"mse"} \tab \code{"gaussian"}, \code{"none"} \cr
   \code{trees} \tab number of trees per patch \tab \code{"poisson"} (a count) \tab \code{"nbinom"}, \code{"mse"}, \code{"none"} \cr
   \code{growth} \tab mean annual relative growth rate \tab \code{"mse"} \tab \code{"gaussian"}, \code{"none"} \cr
   \code{mortality} \tab share of the trees that died, 0 to 1 \tab \code{"binomial"} \tab \code{"mse"}, \code{"gaussian"}, \code{"poisson"}, \code{"none"} \cr
   \code{regeneration} \tab recruits per hectare over the interval \tab \code{"nbinom"} (an overdispersed count) \tab \code{"poisson"}, \code{"mse"}, \code{"gaussian"}, \code{"none"} \cr
}


\code{loss_family} is a named character vector; name only what departs from the
defaults, e.g. \code{loss_family = c(trees = "nbinom")}. \code{"mse"} is the squared
error, equivalent to a Gaussian likelihood with a fixed variance;
\code{"gaussian"} additionally estimates the variance. \code{"binomial"} is the
Bernoulli/binomial likelihood for a proportion in [0, 1] (the model
predicts the probability of dying through a sigmoid), weighted by the
number of trees at risk when \code{data} carries \code{n_at_risk}; it respects the
bounds and the mean-variance link of a proportion, which \code{"mse"} ignores.
\code{"nbinom"} is implemented for \code{trees} and \code{regeneration} only. \code{"none"}
switches a response off: it contributes nothing and needs no column in
\code{data} -- use it when a response was not observed, or when its information
enters through another term (e.g. \code{dbh = "none"} and \code{ba = "none"} once the
size distribution is scored through size classes, below).

The rates are compared over the observation interval: with
\code{period_length} in \code{data}, simulated growth and mortality are averaged
over the interval's steps and simulated recruitment is summed, before they
meet the observation (see \link{finn_data}). Growth is compared as a \emph{relative}
rate (\code{dbh / dbh_before - 1}), the model's own quantity; training against
the absolute increment was tested and was worse and more seed-variable,
because it couples the growth parameters to whichever trees happen to be
present. \code{finn(growth_rate = )} says whether the observed growth is a rate
per time step or the increment over the whole interval.

For \code{regeneration}, \code{"nbinom"} (the default) uses the recruit dispersion
\code{theta} of the regeneration process and applies \code{finn(recruit_obs_weight = )}; \code{"poisson"} has no dispersion parameter, so it cannot collapse, but in
0.2.0 it ignores \code{recruit_obs_weight} and scores per-hectare values
(FINNverse/FINN#65), with \code{trees_offset} added to the prediction. See
\link{recruitment}.

A missing observation (\code{NA}) is left out of its term; the other responses
of that cell are still scored. A term that is not finite at a step (an
overflowing likelihood) is dropped from that step with the default
\code{fit(nonfinite = "drop_term")} and counted in \code{model$nonfinite_counts}.
}

\section{Balancing the six terms (\code{loss_weights}, \code{weight_scale})}{

The six terms are summed, and their raw sizes differ by orders of
magnitude: on the bundled FIA data \code{dbh} is a squared error in cm2 (about
430 at the start) while \code{growth} is a squared error on a ratio (about
0.03). Summed as they are, \code{dbh} would take 87 \% of the objective and
\code{growth} 1 \%, and growth would never improve.

\code{loss_weights = "auto"} (the default) fixes this without knowing the units.
Each term is divided by its \strong{intercept-only baseline}: the loss that
predicting the single best constant (the mean of the observations) would
get. Every term then measures the same thing on the same scale, the
fraction of its own null deviance. For a squared error the baseline is the
variance of the observations, so this is scaling by \code{1 / var(y)}; for the
Poisson, negative-binomial and binomial terms it is the corresponding null
deviance, where a standard deviation would be the wrong scale. The
baselines are stored in \code{model$loss_baseline}, the resulting weights in
\code{model$loss_weights}, and every fit reports each response's share of the
objective at the baseline. It is a weighting for a balanced objective, not
the joint likelihood of the six responses.

A useful side effect: the per-response values in \code{model$history} read
directly as "how much better than the mean" -- \code{1} is no better than the
intercept, below \code{1} is better, above \code{1} is worse.

A numeric \code{loss_weights} vector (six numbers, in the order \code{dbh}, \code{ba},
\code{trees}, \code{growth}, \code{mortality}, \code{regeneration}) multiplies the balanced
terms, so each number reads as that response's relative share:
\code{c(1, 1, 1, 2, 1, 1)} gives \code{growth} twice the weight of every other
response. On the FIA example, balancing the objective is worth about +0.24
Spearman correlation on held-out \code{growth}, more than tuning \code{lr} or
\code{epochs} (vignette \emph{Fitting FINN to FIA data}). \code{weight_scale = FALSE} (or
\code{"raw"}) applies the vector to the raw, unbalanced terms instead -- the
meaning the argument \code{weights} had before 0.2.0, kept for reproducing
earlier analyses; the shares are reported in both modes.
}

\section{How cohorts become the compared quantity (\code{loss_aggregation})}{

The simulation works on cohorts; the observation is one number per site,
year and species. \code{loss_aggregation} says how the cohorts are summarised
before the comparison, per response, as a named character vector or -- for
a type that needs settings -- a named list. Anything not named keeps its
default, so the argument only ever states what departs from it:
\itemize{
\item \code{"mean"} -- the abundance-weighted mean over the cohorts of a site and
species. The default for \code{dbh}, \code{growth} and \code{mortality}.
\item \code{"sum"} -- the sum over cohorts. The default for \code{ba}, \code{trees} and
\code{regeneration}.
\item \code{"size_classes"} -- stem counts per diameter class, for \code{trees} only.
Needs \code{breaks} (the class boundaries in cm) and optionally \code{tau} (the
softness of the class edges in cm, default 1). \code{K} breaks give \code{K + 1}
classes, read from the columns \code{n_class_1}, ..., \code{n_class_K+1} of \code{data}.
With a Poisson family this scores total abundance and size composition at
once (independent Poisson class counts factorise into a Poisson total
times a multinomial composition). Class membership is a difference of
sigmoids rather than a hard cut, so the term stays differentiable in the
diameter; small \code{tau} approaches hard binning.
\item \code{"quantiles"} -- the abundance-weighted diameter quantiles instead of
the mean, for \code{dbh} only. Needs \code{probs} (e.g. \code{c(0.1, 0.5, 0.9)}) and
optionally \code{tau} (the softness on the cumulative-abundance scale,
default 0.05). The observation is read from the columns \code{dbh_q10},
\code{dbh_q50}, \code{dbh_q90} of \code{data} (\verb{dbh_q<100*p>}). Where size classes ask
how many stems fall in an interval, this asks how thick the stem at a
given position of the distribution is: the upper quantile speaks about
the big trees that carry the basal area, which a count likelihood weighs
like saplings.
\item \code{"tree"} -- no aggregation: the growth and mortality equations are
evaluated on the \strong{observed} trees at their observed diameters, with the
light of the observed stand, and compared tree by tree. For \code{growth} and
\code{mortality} only; needs \code{tree_data}. See \emph{Scoring individual trees}.
}

Example -- score the size distribution through stem counts and the two
rates per tree:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{fit(m, data = obs_dt, env = env, tree_data = tree_obs_dt,
    loss_family      = c(dbh = "none", ba = "none"),
    loss_aggregation = list(trees  = list(type = "size_classes",
                                          breaks = seq(20, 60, by = 10)),
                            growth = "tree", mortality = "tree"))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Scoring individual trees (\code{tree_data}, \code{loss_resolution})}{

By default \code{growth} and \code{mortality} are scored per site, species and
census, as means over cohorts. Two options score them per tree instead;
both take the tree table in \code{tree_data} (\link{finn_data} lists its columns),
but they ask different questions.

\code{loss_aggregation = c(growth = "tree", mortality = "tree")} predicts each
OBSERVED tree: the equations run on its measured dbh at the start of the
interval with the light of the observed stand, and only the yearly
environment changes across the interval. It measures the error of the
growth and mortality functions alone. \code{loss_resolution = "tree"} predicts
each SIMULATED cohort that started as an observed tree, along the model's
own trajectory (simulated growth, competitors, light). It measures the
error of the functions plus the drift of the simulated stand.\tabular{lll}{
    \tab \code{loss_aggregation = "tree"} \tab \code{loss_resolution = "tree"} \cr
   the tree is \tab observed: measured dbh, observed competitors, yearly environment \tab simulated: the cohort it started as, along the model's trajectory \cr
   measures \tab the growth and mortality functions alone \tab the functions plus the drift of the simulated stand \cr
   responses \tab chosen separately, e.g. only \code{mortality = "tree"} \tab growth and mortality together \cr
   \code{init_cohort} \tab any, incl. binned (\code{makeInitCohorts(dbh_binsize = )}) \tab one cohort per tree (\code{makeInitCohorts()} without \code{dbh_binsize}) \cr
   \code{tree_data} needs \tab \code{dbh} at the interval start \tab \code{cohortID} of the initial cohort \cr
   combines with \tab the simulated \code{dbh}/\code{ba}/\code{trees}/\code{regeneration} terms \tab \code{reanchor} and \code{teacher_forcing} \cr
   diagnostics \tab \code{\link[=predictTrees]{predictTrees()}} \tab \verb{$tree_deaths}, \verb{$tree_pred} \cr
}


Use the first to estimate the process functions from the trees as
observed, the second to test whether simulated trajectories follow the
real trees. The two cannot be combined in one fit.

Under \code{loss_resolution = "tree"} the two terms \strong{replace} the per-cell
terms rather than adding to them (scoring a response twice under two
weights is a redundancy the optimiser can trade against itself); the loss
vector, \code{loss_weights} and \verb{$history} keep their six positions. The
correspondence is carried by the cohort ids: a cohort keeps the id of the
slot it was created in, so cohort \emph{j} of a (site, patch) is tree \emph{j} of the
list that generation started from; recruits get ids above that block. A
generation starts at \code{init_cohort} and restarts at every anchor under
\code{reanchor}/\code{teacher_forcing}. Growth is scored over the interval as the
aggregate term is, mortality as the compounded probability
\code{1 - prod(1 - m)} against the tree's 0/1 fate -- never the sampled death,
which at one tree is a coin flip. A cohort the simulation kills inside a
window is kept with \code{trees = 0} until its generation ends, so both terms
score every tree and the scored sample does not depend on the parameters;
\verb{$tree_deaths} reports per census and epoch how often the simulation
killed a tree the inventory found alive (\code{frac_killed}), in which year
(\code{mean_kill_year}), and the share that entered the window already dead
(\code{frac_ghost}). With the binned competition a killed cohort still occupies
a slot of the height quantiles, so \code{createProcess(light_mask = TRUE)} (the
default) or \code{continuous = TRUE} is needed for the competition process;
\code{fit()} warns otherwise.
}

\section{Where the recruits come from (\code{loss_recruit})}{

\code{loss_recruit = "simulated"} (the default) lets the regeneration process
recruit, and its parameters are estimated with the rest. An inventory
samples small trees sparsely, so recruitment is the hardest process to
estimate jointly: a mis-fitted regeneration process feeds a wrong stand
into every later interval, and growth and mortality compensate for it.
\code{loss_recruit = "observed"} therefore drives the stand with the OBSERVED
recruitment: \code{reg} in \code{data} divided by \code{period_length} is the expected
recruitment per hectare and year in every simulated year of that
interval, drawn per patch by the recruit sampler as usual. In that call
the regeneration term is weighted 0, every parameter of the regeneration
process is frozen (and gets its previous state back when \code{fit()}
returns), and growth, mortality and competition are estimated on a stand
that recruits what was observed. The regeneration process is then fitted
in a call of its own, with the other processes' \code{optimizeSpecies} and
\code{optimizeEnv} off. Refused: an \code{NA} \code{reg} in an interval that needs it, a
simulated year in two intervals or before a site's first interval, and
\code{reanchor}/\code{teacher_forcing}. \code{\link[=simulateForest]{simulateForest()}} with
\code{recruits = "observed"} simulates the same way for checking such a model;
\link{recruitment} has the unit chain and the staged recipe.
}

\section{Reading the fit}{

\code{model$history} holds the six (weighted) terms per epoch,
\code{model$loss_baseline} the intercept-only baselines, \code{model$loss_weights}
the weights in force, and \code{model$param_history} the parameters at every
\code{checkpoints} epochs. A fit has converged when the parameters stop moving,
not when the losses look flat.
}

